<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iruña-Veleia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Veleia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iruña-Veleia is an archaeological site near Vitoria-Gasteiz in Álava, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the remains of the Roman town of Veleia, which grew from a settlement of the Caristii. It flourished on the road from Asturica to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Burdigala</a> and was walled in the late Roman period. In 2006 inscriptions found there, claimed as the earliest written Basque, were judged to be forgeries, and a court later convicted the excavation director.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iruna-veleia.jpg" alt="Iruña-Veleia" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sugaar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Basque_tribes.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies in the municipality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Iruña</a> de Oca, 10 kilometres west of Vitoria, in the province of Álava in the Basque Autonomous Community. It is the most important site of the Roman period in the Basque Country. The town was a major station on the Roman road ab Asturica Burdigalam, which ran parallel to the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a>, and at its height it may have had some five to ten thousand inhabitants. Since only a small part of it has been excavated, some scholars, among them J. M. Lacarra, have argued that it was the Victoriacum founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Liuvigild</a> in his campaigns against the Vascones in 581.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement was founded in the 8th century BCE, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, with rectangular and round houses of adobe walls and thatched roofs like those at nearby Atxa. In the first half of the 1st century houses in the Roman style began to replace them. The late Roman town of the 3rd and 4th centuries shows signs of decay, and its wall enclosed eleven hectares. The town outlived Roman power into the 5th century, but by its end only burials in abandoned buildings remain. An abbey stood on the site from at least the 16th century until the mid-19th.</p><p>In 2006 the excavation's director, Eliseo Gil, announced finds that included supposed early Basque texts, inscriptions referring to Egyptian history, some in hieroglyphs, and the earliest known depiction of the Calvary. A scientific advisory committee set up by the provincial government of Álava found problems with the finds in every report but Gil's at its meeting of 19 November 2008, and Julio Núñez became the new director of excavations.</p><p>In June 2020 a court found Gil guilty of fraud, ruling that the pieces had been given modern incisions to make them look ancient; his appeal was dismissed and he turned to the Constitutional Court. Gil has always defended his finds. Some scholars from four countries still argue for their authenticity, while the committee members Joaquín Gorrochategui and Juan Santos Yanguas have set out linguistic and epigraphic features they hold to be incompatible with an ancient date.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iruña-Veleia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ptolemy names their towns as Suessatium, Tullica and <b>Veleia</b>, the last of which grew into the Roman town of Iruña-Veleia.</blockquote><a href="/state/caristii">Caristii</a></li></ul></section>
